{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Api-Cloudtrail-2013-11-01","context":"### AdvancedFieldSelector\n\n##### Description\n\nA single selector statement in an advanced event selector.\n\n##### Members\n\n**EndsWith**\n: : **Type:** *Array of strings*\n\n An operator that includes events that match the last few characters of the event record field specified as the value of `Field`.\n\n**Equals**\n: : **Type:** *Array of strings*\n\n An operator that includes events that match the exact value of the event record field specified as the value of `Field`. This is the only valid operator that you can use with the `readOnly`, `eventCategory`, and `resources.type` fields.\n\n**Field**\n: : **Required**: *Yes*\n : **Type:** *string*\n\n A field in a CloudTrail event record on which to filter events to be logged. For event data stores for CloudTrail Insights events, Config configuration items, Audit Manager evidence, or events outside of Amazon Web Services, the field is used only for selecting events as filtering is not supported.\n\n For more information, see AdvancedFieldSelector in the *CloudTrail API Reference*.\n\n Selectors don't support the use of wildcards like `*` . To match multiple values with a single condition, you may use `StartsWith`, `EndsWith`, `NotStartsWith`, or `NotEndsWith` to explicitly match the beginning or end of the event field.\n\n**NotEndsWith**\n: : **Type:** *Array of strings*\n\n An operator that excludes events that match the last few characters of the event record field specified as the value of `Field`.\n\n**NotEquals**\n: : **Type:** *Array of strings*\n\n An operator that excludes events that match the exact value of the event record field specified as the value of `Field`.\n\n**NotStartsWith**\n: : **Type:** *Array of strings*\n\n An operator that excludes events that match the first few characters of the event record field specified as the value of `Field`.\n\n**StartsWith**\n: : **Type:** *Array of strings*\n\n An operator that includes events that match the first few characters of the event record field specified as the value of `Field`","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-sdk-php/v3/api/api-cloudtrail-2013-11-01.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AdvancedEventSelector","context":"## Contents\n\n**FieldSelectors**\n: Contains all selector statements in an advanced event selector.\n\n Type: Array of AdvancedFieldSelector objects\n\n Array Members: Minimum number of 1 item.\n\n Required: Yes\n\n**Name**\n: An optional, descriptive name for an advanced event selector, such as \"Log data events\n for only two S3 buckets\".\n\n Type: String\n\n Length Constraints: Minimum length of 0. Maximum length of 1000.\n\n Pattern: `.*`\n\n Required: No","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/APIReference/API_AdvancedEventSelector.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS CloudTrail Best Practices | AWS Cloud Operations Blog","context":"## Use advanced event selectors with data events\n\nWhen you use data events, advanced event selectors offer more granular control of data event logging. With advanced event selectors, you can include or exclude values on fields such as `EventSource`, `EventName`, and `ResourceARN`. Advanced event selectors also support including or excluding values with pattern matching on partial strings, similar to regular expressions. This provides more control over which CloudTrail data events you want to log and pay for. For example, you can log S3 `DeleteObject` APIs to narrow the CloudTrail events you receive to only destructive actions to identify security issues while controlling costs. Keep in mind that when you use CloudTrail for auditing, it is a best practice to record all data events. However, when you use data events for operational monitoring or other use cases, advanced event selectors can be very helpful","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/aws-cloudtrail-best-practices/"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Selectively log network activity events by identity in AWS CloudTrail - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced enhanced event filtering for network activity events related to Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) endpoints in CloudTrail. This new feature allows customers to selectively log network activity events based on the identity of the IAM user making the API call. Users can configure selectors to log only specific events, such as access denied events, when the calling identity is not on a trusted list. This helps in reducing logging costs and filtering out routine traffic from trusted identities. The UserIdentity filtering feature can be used to focus on network activity event logging for security scenarios and detect potential data exfiltration attempts through VPC endpoints. Users can combine UserIdentity conditions with existing fields like eventName or vpcEndpointId for fine-grained control over what gets recorded. The feature is available via the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface, and AWS SDKs in all AWS Regions where CloudTrail network activity events are supported. For more information, visit the AWS CloudTrail user guide or read the AWS Blog on Network Activity Events","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-cloudtrail-filter-useridentity-advance-selectors/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Uncover blind spots in AWS data plane operations with CloudTrail Event Coverage - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail's new feature, Event Coverage, allows users to view their data plane operations coverage at the account and organization levels in the AWS CloudTrail console. This feature helps identify gaps in logging posture by showing which AWS services and resource types have data event logging enabled and which do not. Data events enable tracking of data plane operations in AWS services, such as Amazon S3 object-level operations, to detect unauthorized access or exfiltration attempts. Event Coverage provides a centralized view of coverage across all supported data event sources and enables subscribing to data events directly from the dashboard. This simplifies closing coverage gaps, especially for organizations managing multiple accounts. Event Coverage is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS CloudTrail is supported. For more information on logging Data Events, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-cloudtrail-event-coverage/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS CloudTrail adds data event aggregation to simplify security monitoring - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service that records API calls for AWS account activity, introduces a new feature called CloudTrail aggregated events. This feature simplifies monitoring and analysis of high-volume CloudTrail data events by consolidating them into 5-minute summaries. Security, compliance, and operations teams can efficiently identify trends like access frequency, error rates, and frequently used actions without processing individual events. Aggregations are available for API activity, resource access, and user activity summaries. Users can enable aggregation through the AWS console or CLI and choose from pre-built templates. The aggregation service is charged based on the number of CloudTrail data events analyzed. It is available in all commercial AWS Regions. With this feature, teams can answer questions like \"How has this user's activity changed over the past week?\" or \"What are the top actions being performed on this critical resource?\" without having to scan through massive amounts of CloudTrail data events","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/cloudtrail-data-event-aggregation-security-monitoring/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudTrail advanced event selector AND/OR logic semantics in docs."}
